I think about privacy on three fronts:

1. Hackability
2. Trackability
3. Culpability

Hackability is how vulnerable you are to being attacked by malicious actors on the internet. This I think should be your first priority as it is the most likely to ruin your life and is done by people who are literally criminals. For this we need to take measures like password managers and isolation between your services. This begins to overlap with front 2.

Trackability is the ominous "big tech". Google, Amazon, Facebook, Microsoft, Apple, etc. This front is secured by not using those services if you can and then limiting what they know if you can't. Don't use Chrome, use Brave. Don't use Mac or Windows, use Linux. Don't use Whatsapp, use Signal. etc.

Culpability is the abilities of government entities to track and target you. For most of us this isn't a real concern. Doing most of the above will cover you here. But this is where you'll want to step up your game to things like connecting to your proton account via Tor.
